        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose This Equipment</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested this equipment. The picks were made from manufacturer
            specification tables, dealer and retailer listings, warranty terms and independent
            reviews, all checked on 16 October 2026. Where we describe what reviewers report, we
            found that reporting.
          </p>
          <p>
            Five rules decided the list. <strong>An established commercial brand:</strong> each
            maker sells into gyms, teams or institutions, not only to homes. <strong>Currently
            sold:</strong> each piece was listed for sale or for quotation on the maker's own site.
            <strong>Agreement:</strong> the core figures had to match between the maker and an
            independent source; the Hammer Strength row's dimensions did not, so they are missing
            from its card. <strong>A published warranty</strong> or a clear route to one in the
            quote. <strong>No current safety action</strong> against the product.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the recall check found</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database by brand and by
            product type. Nothing current applies to the six picks. Several findings matter if you
            are buying in this market, especially used:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Matrix treadmills, power cords.</strong> Johnson Health Tech recalled Matrix
            T1 and T3 series commercial treadmills in January 2022 because the power cord can work
            loose from the socket, posing a fire hazard, and expanded that recall in April 2024. In
            January 2026 the recall was expanded again to include Matrix Retail T30, TF30, T50, TF50
            and T75 treadmills and Vision T9800, TF20, T40 and T80 treadmills — home models sold
            through specialty retailers, made between June 2007 and January 2024. The remedy is a
            free replacement locking power cord; until it is fitted, the CPSC notice says to make sure
            the cord is fully inserted before each use. Our
            <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix Fitness</a>
            page covers the brand.</li>
            <li><strong>Matrix training cycles.</strong> In January 2025 certain Matrix-brand
            indoor cycles sold mainly to facilities were recalled because the seat can drop during
            use; the notice tells users to stop using them until a repair kit is fitted.</li>
            <li><strong>Precor Resolute cable multi-station.</strong> Recalled in December 2024
            because a pop-pin can fail to engage and let the pulley carriage drop; sold to gyms,
            with a stop-use instruction until repaired.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack RW900 rower.</strong> Recalled in October 2025 for a console fire
            hazard. A home machine, but it turns up in small facilities and on the used market.</li>
            <li><strong>Life Fitness</strong> recalled its PowerMill climber in 2018 for a fire
            hazard; we found nothing against the Integrity+ or Hammer Strength row. We found no
            recalls for Keiser or Concept2, and Rogue's appear to cover only older accessories.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Recalls on commercial equipment are often handled by contacting known facility owners
            directly, which means a machine bought second-hand may never have been fixed. Check the
            serial number of any used piece against the CPSC database and the maker before you pay.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-commercial-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What "Commercial" Actually Means</h2>
<p>
            There is no single legal definition of commercial fitness equipment. In practice, the
            word means equipment designed and warrantied for use by many people, for many hours a
            day, in a setting where it will be serviced on a schedule. Four things separate it from
            home equipment that merely borrows the word.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Duty cycle</h3>
          <p>
            A home treadmill is designed around one or two users and perhaps an hour or two a day.
            A commercial treadmill is designed to run most of the day with a queue of users of every
            size. That shows up in the motor (AC drives with a continuous rating rather than a
            headline peak), in the deck and rollers, and in the user-weight ratings — the <a href="/life-fitness-integrity-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Life
            Fitness Integrity</a>+ is rated to 400 lb. The same logic applies to strength equipment:
            heavier steel, bigger hardware, upholstery that tolerates daily cleaning. Our
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill for home</a>
            guide explains how duty translates into the treadmill's components.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Serviceability</h3>
          <p>
            Commercial equipment is built to be repaired, not replaced. Parts are stocked for years,
            technicians are trained on the models, and the frames are designed so that a belt,
            bearing or cable can be swapped on site. Before you buy, ask who services the equipment
            in your area, how quickly, and for how many years parts will be available. A commercial
            machine with no local service is a heavy, expensive home machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Documentation</h3>
          <p>
            Commercial makers publish installation requirements — power, clearances, anchoring —
            and maintenance schedules. Those documents matter more than any brochure, because they
            are what a warranty claim, an insurer or an inspector will be measured against.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How it is sold</h3>
          <p>
            Much commercial equipment is sold through dealers and by quote, with delivery,
            installation and sometimes a service plan bundled in. A price on request is normal, not a
            warning sign; the quote is where you negotiate the warranty, the installation and the
            service terms. Some makers also sell the same or similar equipment through home stores at
            published prices, as Life Fitness does — useful for comparison, but read which warranty
            applies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranties" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Commercial and Home Warranties Are Different Contracts</h2>
<p>
            The same machine can carry two quite different warranties depending on where it is used
            and who bought it. Treat the warranty as part of the specification, and get the version
            that applies to you in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Home warranties usually assume home use.</strong> Read whether the terms cover a
            machine used by several households, by clients or by employees. Many home warranties are
            written for residential use only, and using the machine commercially can shorten or end
            the cover. If you are equipping a studio or a shared gym with home-sold equipment, ask the
            seller to confirm in writing that the warranty still applies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial terms are often shorter on paper and stronger in practice.</strong> A
            home warranty may promise a lifetime on the frame and years on parts, because a home
            machine sees little use. A commercial warranty assumes heavy use and is often structured
            around parts and labour from a service network. As an example of how terms are published,
            Life Fitness's home store lists the Integrity treadmill it sells there with a lifetime
            frame and motor warranty, ten years on parts and one year on labour, and lists the Hammer
            Strength Iso-Lateral Row with ten years on the frame, five on bearings and 90 days on
            hardware, grips and upholstery. Facility buyers should expect their own terms in the quote.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour is where the money goes.</strong> On a commercial treadmill, a technician
            visit can cost more than the part. A long parts warranty with a short labour warranty is
            worth less than it looks; ask about service contracts and response times. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read the small print, and most of it applies to any machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance is usually a condition.</strong> Commercial warranties typically
            require the maker's <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>maintenance schedule</a> to be followed and recorded. Keep a log from day
            one: date, machine, serial number, what was done and by whom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Power, Floors and Space</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Commercial treadmills often need a dedicated 20-amp circuit.</strong> Life
            Fitness specifies a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit for the Integrity+ treadmill, and
            its home store says the Integrity treadmill sold there needs a 120 V, 20 A outlet on a
            dedicated line with a NEMA 5 plug. A 20-amp plug has one blade turned sideways and will not
            fit an ordinary 15-amp receptacle, and a standard household circuit shared with other
            sockets is the wrong thing to run it from. Always follow the installation requirements for
            the exact model, and have a licensed electrician check or install the circuit; do not use
            an extension cord or adapter. Other cardio pieces here are simpler: Concept2's RowErg
            monitor runs on two D-cell batteries, so no outlet is needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floors.</strong> Commercial equipment is heavy. The Hammer Strength row weighs
            280 lb before any plates, and a loaded rack with a lifter, bar and plates puts a great
            deal of weight on a small footprint. On a concrete garage or ground floor that is rarely a
            problem; upstairs, in an apartment building or on a suspended office floor it can be. That
            is a question for a structural engineer or the building's management, not for a product
            page. Use rubber flooring or a platform under strength equipment, both to protect the floor
            and to reduce noise — see our guide to
            <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platforms</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Space.</strong> Plan for the machine plus its use clearance. Concept2 recommends a
            9 by 4 ft area to use the RowErg, against an 8 by 2 ft machine. Treadmills need clear space
            behind the deck so a user who falls is not trapped against a wall; the maker's installation
            guide gives the figure. Racks need overhead clearance for pull-ups and for pressing — the
            RML-390C's tall uprights are 90 inches, before the lifter's arms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Safe Use of Commercial Strength Equipment</h2>
<p>
            Commercial strength equipment is built to take abuse, but most injuries around it come
            from set-up and habits rather than failures. A few rules apply to every facility,
            including a one-person garage.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Rated capacities are manufacturer figures.</strong> They describe the
            equipment under the maker's own conditions, not a guarantee for every use. Rogue does not
            publish a load rating for the RML-390C on its product page, which is common for racks; it
            does say the rack must be bolted to the floor. Treat any capacity as a ceiling, not a
            target.</li>
            <li><strong>Anchor or bolt as the maker specifies.</strong> A rack that must be bolted
            down is not safe free-standing, especially with band pegs or spotter arms loaded off-centre.
            Use the anchors the maker specifies for your floor.</li>
            <li><strong>Use safeties every time.</strong> Set pin-and-pipe safeties or spotter arms
            just below the bottom of the lift for squats and presses inside the rack. In a shared
            facility, make it a rule and put it on the wall.</li>
            <li><strong>Check floor loading for heavy racks upstairs.</strong> As above: general
            guidance only, and a structural engineer is the person to ask about an unusual floor.</li>
            <li><strong>Inspect on a schedule.</strong> Bolts, pins, pulleys and upholstery wear.
            Record inspections, take faulty equipment out of service with a visible sign, and repair
            it with the maker's parts.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a deeper look at racks, including safety systems and how to choose one for a home
            space, see our guide to the
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best squat racks for home gyms</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="liability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Facilities: Liability, Insurance and Records</h2>
<p>
            The moment equipment is used by people other than you — clients, residents, employees,
            members — the questions change. This section is general and not legal or insurance
            advice; take professional advice for your situation.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Talk to your insurer before you buy.</strong> Insurers may have views on
            equipment type, supervision, waivers, signage and maintenance records, and some prefer
            commercial-rated equipment. Find out before you commit to a list.</li>
            <li><strong>Get legal advice on waivers and access rules.</strong> An apartment building or
            office gym raises questions about who may use it, when, and on what terms.</li>
            <li><strong>Follow the installation documents.</strong> Power, clearances, anchoring and
            signage requirements from the maker are the baseline anyone reviewing an incident will look
            at.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep records.</strong> Purchase documents, serial numbers, installation
            sign-off, maintenance logs and recall checks. They protect the warranty and the owner.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan for the unsupervised hour.</strong> Many small facilities are open when no
            staff are present. Emergency stops, safety keys on treadmills, clear instructions and a way
            to call for help matter more there than in a staffed gym.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this is a reason to avoid running a small gym. It is a reason to budget time and
            money for the parts that are not equipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Buying Used or Refurbished Commercial Equipment</h2>
<p>
            Used commercial equipment is one of the better bargains in fitness, because gyms replace
            machines on a cycle rather than when they fail, and well-built equipment has years left in
            it. It is also where most of the risk sits. Our guide to
            <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills</a>
            covers the treadmill case in detail; the principles carry across.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Know the three grades.</strong> "Used" or "as is" means sold as found.
            "Serviced" usually means cleaned and checked. "Remanufactured" or "refurbished" should mean
            wear parts replaced, with a written warranty. Ask exactly what was replaced.</li>
            <li><strong>Check recalls by serial number.</strong> As the Matrix power-cord recall shows,
            commercial recalls are often handled facility by facility, and a resold machine can slip
            through.</li>
            <li><strong>Confirm parts support.</strong> An older commercial model may be out of
            production; check the maker or a service company still supplies its parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Count the hidden costs.</strong> Freight, stairs, installation, a dedicated
            circuit for a treadmill, and the fact that the original warranty usually does not
            transfer.</li>
            <li><strong>Strength equipment ages better.</strong> A plate-loaded machine or rack has
            few wear parts beyond bearings, pins and upholstery, which makes it a safer used buy than
            an electronic treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a>
            gives the inspection steps, and the
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill for home</a>
            guide covers whether an ex-gym machine suits a house at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-setting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Garage, Studio, Building or Office: What to Prioritise</h2>
<p>
            The same six pieces suit very different settings in different orders. A rough guide:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A garage gym built to commercial standard.</strong> Start with the rack, flooring
            and dumbbells, because they last decades and are the hardest to upgrade later. A rower is
            the obvious cardio piece: no outlet, small storage footprint, near-indestructible. A
            commercial treadmill only makes sense if you can supply the circuit and get it through the
            door.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A small studio.</strong> Buy for the classes you run. Indoor cycles and rowers are
            the core of many small studios because they are compact and need no power; a rack or two
            and a <a href="/best-dumbbell-sets/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>dumbbell set</a> cover strength classes. Budget for service contracts from the start.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An apartment building.</strong> Unsupervised use, residents of every size and
            experience, and floors above other people's homes. Favour equipment with simple controls,
            built-in safety features and low noise; check floor loading and noise transmission before
            installing heavy strength equipment; and keep the maintenance log religiously.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An office gym.</strong> Usually short, busy windows before and after work and at
            lunch. Cardio dominates; a treadmill, a bike and a rower cover most people. Selectorized or
            plate-loaded machines are easier for occasional users than free weights.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the setting, the brands behind our treadmill pick have their own pages on this
            site: see <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a>
            for the brand's commercial and home lines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. When Home-Grade Equipment Is the Better Buy</h2>
<p>
            Commercial equipment is not automatically better for everyone. It is heavier, larger,
            pricier and, for a treadmill, more demanding to install.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If one or two people will use it,</strong> a good home machine with a long warranty
            will usually serve as well for far less, and fit through the door. Our
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best cardio machines for home</a>
            guide covers the strongest home options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you cannot provide a dedicated 20-amp circuit,</strong> a commercial
            treadmill is the wrong purchase until you can. Look at home treadmills that run on a
            standard circuit, or put the budget into a rower or bike.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are short of space,</strong> adjustable dumbbells and a compact rack may
            beat a full commercial set; see our guide to
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>.
            For a single machine that does much of what a plate-loaded row does, look at our
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machines guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a health condition,</strong> the right equipment depends on advice
            from a qualified professional, not on its grade. We review equipment; this is not medical
            advice. For the wider picture, start at our
            <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym hub</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between commercial and home gym equipment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Commercial equipment is designed, warrantied and serviced for many users and long daily hours. That usually means heavier frames, motors with continuous ratings, parts that can be replaced on site, published installation requirements and dealer service. Home equipment is designed around one household. The word commercial on a home product listing does not by itself mean any of this, so check the warranty and specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do commercial treadmills need a special outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many do. Life Fitness, for example, specifies a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit for its Integrity+ treadmill, and a 20-amp plug will not fit an ordinary 15-amp socket. Check the installation requirements for the exact model before you buy, and have a licensed electrician install or confirm the circuit. Do not use an extension cord or adapter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use commercial gym equipment at home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if your home can take it. The common obstacles are a dedicated 20-amp circuit for a treadmill, the weight on an upstairs floor, doorways and stairs for delivery, and ceiling height for racks. Also check which warranty applies when commercial equipment is used at home, because terms can differ from those offered to facilities.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is used commercial gym equipment worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, particularly strength equipment, which has few wear parts. Treadmills and electronic cardio carry more risk. Buy from a seller who says exactly what was replaced and gives a written warranty, check the serial number against CPSC recalls, confirm parts are still available, and budget for freight, installation and power. The original warranty usually does not transfer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do commercial gym equipment makers not show prices?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Facility equipment is usually sold through dealers by quote, with delivery, installation, warranty terms and service plans negotiated together. Prices also vary with consoles, finishes and quantities. Some makers publish prices for the same or similar equipment on home stores, which is useful for comparison. A price on request is normal in this market, not a warning sign.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need insurance for an apartment or office gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost certainly some form of cover is involved, but the details depend on the building, the users and your location, so speak to an insurer or broker before you buy equipment. They may have requirements about equipment type, supervision, signage, waivers and maintenance records. Legal advice on access rules and waivers is also worth taking. This is general information, not advice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Commercial Gym Equipment</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Commercial equipment rarely fails its buyers by breaking. It fails them by not fitting the room, the circuit, the warranty or the service arrangements they assumed.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a commercial treadmill before checking the circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many commercial treadmills need a dedicated 20-amp circuit, and the plug will not fit an ordinary socket. People discover this on delivery day. Read the installation requirements before you order, have an electrician confirm or install the circuit, and never run the machine from an extension cord or adapter while you wait.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the warranty covers how you use it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A home warranty may not cover a machine used by clients, residents or employees, and a facility warranty may be conditional on scheduled maintenance. Ask which warranty applies to your setting, get it in writing with the quote, and keep a maintenance log from the first day so you can show the conditions were met.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying used without a recall or parts check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Commercial recalls are often handled by contacting facility owners directly, so a resold machine may never have been fixed, as the Matrix power-cord recall shows. Check every serial number against the CPSC database and the maker, confirm parts are still supplied, and ask exactly what a refurbisher replaced before you pay.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating rated capacity as a target</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A rack, bench or machine capacity is the maker's figure under its own conditions, and many racks publish none. Bolt racks down as the maker specifies, use safeties or spotter arms on every heavy set, and get advice on floor loading before installing heavy strength equipment upstairs. The safest set-up is the one that never relies on the maximum.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

